{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012swfc1Kyc9mgkVvUwGaxdH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wait, I'm changing behavior here. Previously `renderPreloadLinks` received `ctx.modules` from Vue's SSR context, which could have actual module IDs. Now I'm passing `[]`. This would break preload link generation for users who pass a manifest.\n\nLet me reconsider. The `manifest` feature is important for production builds. I need to preserve it. Let me look at this differently.\n\nThe Vue renderer's `renderToString` method currently doesn't return module information. But it's an internal implementation. I can enhance the Vue renderer to support a callback or option that captures the SSR context modules. Or I can take a different approach entirely.\n\nActually, the simplest correct approach: `getRender` should still use the Vue renderer for the core SSR logic but needs access to `ctx.modules`. I can do this by having `getRender` NOT use `getRendererRender`, but instead directly call the renderer's `renderToString` and wrap it with preload link handling. But `renderToString` returns just a string...\n\nThe real issue is that preload link generation is tightly coupled with Vue's SSR context, which the abstract renderer interface intentionally hides. This is a design tension.\n\nThe pragmatic solution: the `FrameworkRenderer.renderToString` interface already says the return value \"may include preload links separated by `<!-- preload -->`\". So the Vue renderer's `renderToString` should handle preload links if given a manifest. Let me add manifest support to `VueRendererOptions`:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":81966,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":926},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_